# Checked CUDA lowering for Tix

Emit CUDA C directly from the selected function in `program.current`. Check the emitted translation unit by running it on the GB10 and comparing its returned tensor with the original Tix function evaluated in f64. The check is sampled. CUDA's single-precision transcendental functions can disagree with the stage-1 f32 evaluator by a bit even on small, finite inputs.

**As built in c7824d4 and 6f94de2.** The sole framework source change is the batch-only specification in `src/framework/contracts/differential.ts`. A union parameter implements the planned async overload, with runtime guards for synchronous and worker-side evaluation. The runner uses a UUID-named private job and a per-key compile lock. Concurrent cold checks, a genuine split and a compiled dropped-tail mutant exercised these paths on the GB10. [Implementation and measurement report](../reference/02-cuda-lowering-results-and-framework-findings.md)

## Why this relation

The untimed probe compiled with the existing runner's `nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_121`. The device identified itself as NVIDIA GB10, nvcc 13.0.88. For each of 32,769 exactly representable values `x = i/4096`, from −4 through 4 inclusive, a kernel evaluated the six expressions below. Node 24.18.0 ran matching Tix functions through both stage-1 evaluators on identical f32 inputs. Here `p = abs(x) + 0.125`. The last column divides each error by the stage-1 budget at that coordinate and takes the maximum. [Kernel](../scripts/01-gb10/main.cu) · [Tix comparison](../scripts/02-compare.mjs)

| Device expression | Words differing from stage-1 f32 | Largest absolute error vs f64 | Largest fraction of budget |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `expf(x)` | 9,840 | 4.864e−6 | 0.00668 |
| `logf(p)` | 2,786 | 1.238e−7 | 0.00354 |
| `__fsqrt_rn(p)` | 0 | 1.191e−7 | 0.00283 |
| `tanhf(x)` | 5,058 | 8.567e−8 | 0.00663 |
| Ordered sum of `x * ((j−2)/7)`, j = 0…6 | 0 | 4.769e−7 | 0.00663 |
| `(x−p)/p` | 0 | 5.961e−8 | 0.00269 |

At `x = -3.998291015625`, the GPU returned `0x3c964c60` for exp and the f32 evaluator returned `0x3c964c61`. This rules out the exact relation for the proposed emitter. Matching the host math implementation on device would cost substantially more than using libdevice. Adopt the stage-1 output budget for every finite reference coordinate r,

```text
abs(gpu − r) ≤ 2e−6 + 2e−5 × abs(r)
```

The absolute term covers cancellation near zero. The relative term scales with output magnitude. All 196,614 comparisons fit, including the ordered fold. This measures headroom on the probe grid. Deep expressions, long sums and ill-conditioned programs can exceed the budget. Each function must earn its own verdict on its generated cases. The large benchmarks measure speed separately.

## Supported programs and calls

The input is a stage-1 `TixProgram` from `compileTix` or `optimizeTix(...).program` and one of its function names. Calls use finite f32 data in row-major storage, one buffer per parameter with the declared rank, matching length and consistent nonnegative dimensions. A plain parameter axis binds each shape symbol. Affine dimensions and addresses are integral, representable as in stage 1 and in bounds at the call. Tensors and launch dimensions must fit on the GB10. Zero extents, returned parameters, multiple functions, shadowed binder spellings and the generated split bounds are all within scope. Finite inputs can produce infinities, NaNs or signed zero.

Use the following three exports from `src/tensor/index.ts`, hence from `dist/tensor/index.js` after build.

| Export | Work performed |
| --- | --- |
| `cudaSource(program, fn): string` | Deterministic, shape-parametric translation unit with all statement kernels and a runnable main for this function. |

- The sole framework edit is in src/framework/contracts/differential.ts. BatchDifferentialSpec requires decide and carries the same case and cache keys as a local spec. A union at the async entry point suffices for the proposed overload. Explicit guards reject a batch-only spec passed to sync evaluation or worker-side decideCase. The existing memo retains the baseline references in the generated cases and replays verdicts in case order.
- The focused test exercised partial and full memo hits, a truncated response, a failed batch, retry of the same two missing indices and ordered counterexamples. The real checker accepted all six corpus functions before and after optimization. Concurrent cold checks succeeded. A repeat of the same function replayed its cases without a fresh dispatch.
- The unsplit seven-element witness returned 0x3f800000, the split witness 0x3f800002. Replacing ceiling block count by floor in the emitted source triggered a postcondition rejection with a positive-stream counterexample. The other direct GPU checks cover empty sums/maxima, empty outputs, returned parameters, nested binder scope, transposed inlining, zero signs and NaN propagation.
- The tensor command passed 29/29, the framework command passed 227/227 and the six selected Typed files (meaning-memo, parallel, passes, program, mutate and strategies) passed 37/37.
- Git initially reported a read-only index lock while staging the emitter. The approved git add/commit commands completed the checkpoint as c7824d4. No source changes were lost. The reviewed design revision is 7b3dc67.

Review the actual postcondition in src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts alongside the dropped-tail test in tests/tensor/cuda-gpu.test.mjs. The comparator reports numerical failures. A tool or identity failure rejects the promise before the framework can cache the batch.

The standalone runner probe forced two simultaneous compilations to contend for a fresh full-input key. For key a3ab8cb9c23a302a exactly one run compiled, the other reused the binary, and a third invocation also hit the cache. All three returned the original GB10 probe fingerprint. The checker records its target profile and translation digest with the evidence.

## Step 5. Measure all six programs and close the ticket

At implementation revision 6f94de2, the build and the final 29 tensor tests completed successfully. The final emitter passes only referenced tensor pointers and shape scalars to each kernel. The same final build produced a four-case timed batch for each original corpus program. The benchmark driver reports one warm-up and three subsequent CUDA event intervals. Each requested local interpreter call also finished. Across the six measured workloads the GB10 medians ranged from 0.1700 to 0.9924 ms. Interpreter calls on the shared host ranged from 15.317 to 812.828 seconds.

The additional Typed differential consumers in evaluate-analyses, tape, specialize, f32, bytecode-vm and candela passed 22 tests. The suite skipped its opt-in Candela O2 workload. Combined with the previous Typed run, 59 passed and one skipped. Importing the built tensor entry point returned exactly checkLowering, compileTix, cudaSource, optimizeTix, runTix and runTixCuda.

Reproduce the measurements from the repository root with the ticket's scripts/04-benchmark.mjs, passing cpu or gpu. Both modes construct the same seed-20 inputs. The CPU mode uses the stage-1 interpreter and can take many minutes. The raw results preserve all six shapes and all 18 measured GPU intervals. [Results, commands and framework findings](02-cuda-lowering-results-and-framework-findings.md)

